# NexGuard L7 ZTNA CoreDNS — single (or HA pair) upstream, cache-heavy (ADR-012).
#
# Two env knobs (the second is optional):
#
#   COREDNS_FORWARD_TO           — primary DNS server. Handles
#                                   everything not in the L7 hosts
#                                   file (typically your corporate
#                                   internal DNS, e.g. 10.0.235.250).
#                                   Default 1.1.1.1.
#
#   COREDNS_FORWARD_TO_FALLBACK  — secondary, only used when primary
#                                   fails the health check. Set when
#                                   you have an HA bind9 pair (e.g.
#                                   10.0.235.251). Defaults to the
#                                   primary, so leaving it unset just
#                                   means "no failover" — no harm.
#
# `policy sequential` (below) sticks to the primary while it's
# healthy so the cache stays hot on one box; fail-over happens only
# when health checks fail, not on every query.
#
# Design goals (in order):
#
#   1. Client experience is never affected by bind9 latency or
#      outages. `serve_stale 1h immediate` keeps replies fast even
#      when the upstream is sick.
#
#   2. Spam to bind9 is minimised. Three layers cut load:
#        - template plugin drops mDNS-style noise (.local/.lan/...)
#          before it leaves the gateway,
#        - aggressive positive + negative caching catches the rest,
#        - prefetch refreshes popular entries before TTL expiry so
#          cache hits never become cache misses for clients.
#
#   3. CoreDNS can't DoS bind9. `max_concurrent 100` and the
#      forward `expire 30s` cap parallel sockets and idle conns.
#
# Bind9-side tuning (out of scope for this file but recommended):
# raise `recursive-clients` past 1000, enable `prefetch`, watch
# `recursing-file` and `rcode-failure` under sustained load.

. {
  # 1. Drop mDNS-style noise BEFORE upstream. Apple Bonjour,
  # Windows WPAD, Linux avahi, browser captive-portal probes all
  # generate guaranteed-NXDOMAIN queries on .local / .lan / .home /
  # .internal / .corp suffixes. Returning NXDOMAIN locally saves
  # ~5–15% of bind9 traffic — the queries never leave the box.
  template ANY ANY local lan home internal corp {
    rcode NXDOMAIN
  }

  # 2. L7 ZTNA VIPs from the hosts file. MUST run before forward,
  # and MUST keep `fallthrough .` so sister names under declared
  # internal zones (e.g. grafana.<your-zone> when only
  # hq.<your-zone> is L7-onboarded) still reach the upstream.
  # Without fallthrough the hosts plugin claims authority over the
  # whole zone and NXDOMAINs every non-L7 sibling.
  hosts /etc/nexguard/internal-hosts {
    reload 1s
    ttl 30
    fallthrough .
  }

  # 3. Upstream — primary + optional HA fallback. `policy sequential`
  # keeps every query on the primary while it's healthy so the cache
  # warms on ONE box (vs. random load-balance which splits the cache
  # and roughly doubles miss rate). Fallback only kicks in when
  # health_check sees the primary down. `max_concurrent 100` is the
  # load-flood backstop — CoreDNS queues beyond 100 in-flight queries
  # rather than open 1000 parallel sockets on burst. `expire 30s`
  # recycles idle upstream conns.
  forward . {$COREDNS_FORWARD_TO} {$COREDNS_FORWARD_TO_FALLBACK} {
    max_concurrent 100
    health_check 5s
    expire 30s
    policy sequential
  }

  # 4. The biggest load-shedder. `serve_stale 1h immediate` makes
  # bind9 latency / outages INVISIBLE to clients: cache returns
  # stale data instantly while CoreDNS refreshes async in the
  # background. `prefetch` keeps popular entries warm so TTL
  # expiry never causes a thundering herd. Capacities are
  # generous; memory cost is ~3–5 MB.
  cache {
    success 16384 3600 60
    denial 4096 300 30
    serve_stale 1h immediate
    prefetch 10 1m 10%
  }

  errors
  log . "{remote}:{port} {>id} {type} {name} -> {rcode} {>rflags} {rsize} {duration}"
}
